{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Frost Flowers: Halophiles of the Arctic Sea-Ice Surface",
    "date": "2026-09-18",
    "lesson": "When young Arctic sea ice forms in still, cold air, delicate ice crystals called frost flowers bloom on its surface, wicking up hypersaline brine from the ice below. These crystals become tiny, extremely salty, extremely cold refuges, seeded with bacteria and aerosolized outward by wind - a major source of marine microbes to polar air. Halophilic and psychrophilic bacteria trapped there must run enzymes and transport in near-frozen, near-saturated brine, and their EPS both protects them from ice-crystal damage and helps hold liquid brine pockets open within the frost flower lattice. The lesson: extreme cold does not have to mean extreme scarcity of salt or structure - it can mean the opposite, a tiny frozen desert of concentrated brine, gripping ice and stubborn life.",
    "citation": "Bowman & Deming (2010) FEMS Microbiol Ecol - frost flower bacterial communities; Barber et al. (2014) Cold Reg. Sci. Technol.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Frost flower brine microbial communities on new Arctic sea ice, Beaufort/Chukchi region"
  },
